render: avoid undefined behaviour on extreme numeric input (CVE-2026-95127)

Subtitles with extreme numbers (huge PlayResX/Y, Fontsize, margins, or
\pos/\fscx/\frx/\fax override values) pushed the renderer's 32-bit and
float-to-int arithmetic out of range: ass_outline_construct() overflowed a
signed subtraction, and the glyph, pen and collision accumulations overflowed
too. That is undefined behaviour, and the published CVE is a denial of
service through it. Integer cases are now computed in 64 bits and wrapped
back to 32 bits (wrap_i32), and float-to-int conversions go through dtoi32(),
so the output matches the x86/VSFilter result without the UB.

libass PR 955 (https://github.com/libass/libass/pull/955), head commit
d570c2f5ed55844ec9b77b085deb3cb8ef59cb4a, fixing issue 947. The PR is not yet
merged. It is applied ahead of a libass release because 0.17.5 is the newest
release and no released version has the fix. This is the revised version of
the PR: review asked for wraparound rather than saturation, to match VSFilter,
and the author reworked it that way (verified byte-identical bitmaps on 1715
corpus inputs).

